===============================================================================
TECHNIQUES, PRIOR WORK AND DEBTS
===============================================================================

3D Gaussian Splatting
    The representation this whole tool rests on.
    Kerbl, B., Kopanas, G., Leimkühler, T. and Drettakis, G. (2023) "3D
    Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering". ACM
    Transactions on Graphics 42(4). doi:10.1145/3592433

The cloth simulation filter
    The ground filter in project/tools/ground_dem.py is written from the paper's
    description, not vendored:
    Zhang, W., Qi, J., Wan, P., Wang, H., Xie, D., Wang, X. and Yan, G. (2016)
    "An Easy-to-Use Airborne LiDAR Data Filtering Method Based on Cloth
    Simulation". Remote Sensing 8(6):501. doi:10.3390/rs8060501
    Its documented weakness on low, dense vegetation is stated wherever the tool
    offers a terrain model.

Umeyama alignment
    The pose regression test aligns two camera paths with the closed-form
    least-squares similarity of:
    Umeyama, S. (1991) "Least-squares estimation of transformation parameters
    between two point patterns". IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and
    Machine Intelligence 13(4):376-380. doi:10.1109/34.88573

The interface grammar and the house theme
    From the sibling tools DL-TerrainDiversity and DL-TerrainMapper (Apache-2.0):
    the floating window, the two-level folds and the design tokens. This tool
    commits to a DARK palette of the same tokens, because splats read best
    against a dark stage.
    https://github.com/DLandscapes/dl-terrain-diversity
    https://github.com/DLandscapes/dl-terrain-mapper

The terrain-model hand-off
    Its GeoTIFFs are written to be opened unchanged by DL-TerrainSlicer, which
    cuts them into laser sheets.
    https://github.com/DLandscapes/dl-terrain-slicer

===============================================================================
DATA
===============================================================================

This repository contains NO captured site data, except the samples named in
project/data/samples/samples.json, if any. The contour landform and the
measuring test in project/data/ are synthesised by the tool itself
(project/tools/make_demo.mjs), and the pose references in
project/tools/reference/ are camera positions only — numbers, no imagery.

Captures, and everything made from them, are deliberately kept out of version
control: footage of a site can show people, and a scene made from it shows
them too. A sample is Digital Landscapes' own material, added by name only
once it has been checked for exactly that. A photograph sample is made with
the Depth Anything V2 Metric Base checkpoint and carries its terms (above).

